WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.472
Hi, I'm Sill!

00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:05.268
This project turns a phone scan of a room into a

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:06.285
scene made of code.

00:00:10.558 --> 00:00:14.091
The scan gives measurements, the photos give looks, and the agent checks

00:00:14.091 --> 00:00:16.446
its work from where each photo was taken.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.060
The phone app records four things.

00:00:23.711 --> 00:00:27.808
Apple's room scanner gives walls, doors, windows and furniture boxes.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:30.839
The depth sensor builds a mesh of every surface.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:33.785
Photos, each with its exact position and lens.

00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:36.431
And small depth images, several a second.

00:00:37.608 --> 00:00:40.755
The app picks its own photos, scoring every mesh point by how

00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:41.804
well it's been seen.

00:00:42.224 --> 00:00:45.890
Pixels per centimetre: near and square on wins, up to about four.

00:00:46.311 --> 00:00:48.766
Times sharpness, predicted from the phone's motion.

00:00:49.358 --> 00:00:50.538
Times an occlusion check.

00:00:50.538 --> 00:00:52.602
It shoots where the score rises most.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:58.264
Take a photo, and put a three.js camera at its exact pose.

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:00.019
Render the model from there.

00:01:01.884 --> 00:01:03.974
A script pastes the photo beside the render.

00:01:03.974 --> 00:01:05.280
It does not score them.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.879
The agent looks, names the biggest difference, and fixes the code.

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:10.129
Then renders again.

00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:12.066
When to stop is a judgement call.

00:01:13.758 --> 00:01:16.111
This is the shipped office, rendered on my machine.

00:01:16.111 --> 00:01:17.941
None of it is a scanned mesh.

00:01:18.361 --> 00:01:22.289
The desk, monitors and chair are code the agent wrote, sized from the scan.

00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:30.216
1,203,514 triangles in the shipped office, from code the agent wrote.

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:34.881
The clever part is the capture: every surface gets a sharp photo

00:01:34.881 --> 00:01:37.650
at a known pose, so a simple loop can work.

00:01:40.608 --> 00:01:44.511
Scan for size, photos for looks, and check from the photo's own camera.

00:01:45.208 --> 00:01:47.317
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:47.920
I'm Sill.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.221
Bye!
